  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes four visible patterns of antibody recognition by different B cells, emphasizing how reactivity can range from broad to highly selective. In each case, a B cell gives rise to antibodies, which then bind an antigen carrying different structural features.\n\nThe left side shows “promiscuous” ACPAs, suggesting antibodies that recognize citrullinated antigens in a broad, less restricted way. Next, “private specific” ACPAs appear to bind a narrower combination of antigen features, indicating strong specificity for a particular citrullinated target. The third category, “private cross-reactive” ACPAs, seems to recognize citrulline together with multiple surrounding non-citrulline residues, implying cross-reactivity among related antigens. The final group, “joint-reactive antibodies,” is distinct in that binding appears directed toward triple-helical epitopes rather than citrulline alone.\n\nAcross the figure, the antigen cartoons include symbols for non-citrulline residues, citrulline, and triple-helical epitopes, highlighting that antibody binding depends not just on citrulline but also on neighboring sequence or structural context. Overall, the message is that antibody responses are heterogeneous: some are broadly citrulline-reactive, some are clone-specific, some cross-react with related targets, and some recognize joint-specific structural motifs.",
